Git Commit Message Generation Rules

Applies to the Source Control (SCM) AI button, `git commit`, and any commit message written for this repository. Follow every step. No exceptions.

1. What to describe: STAGED FIRST, decided by one command

Run exactly this first:

    git diff --cached --name-only

A. The output is NOT empty -> STAGED MODE. This is the common case.
   - Describe ONLY the staged diff: `git diff --cached` (hunks that are staged; a file with both staged and unstaged edits counts only for its staged hunks).
   - Do NOT run `git diff` without `--cached`, `git status`, `git stash`, or `git add`.
   - Do NOT read files from the working tree, open editor tabs, or earlier chat messages to learn what changed. They show unstaged work that must not appear.
   - Unstaged and untracked files do not exist for this message, even when they look related to the staged ones.

B. The output is empty -> ALL MODE.
   - Describe every pending change: `git diff HEAD` for tracked files, plus each untracked file from `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` (read their contents; they are part of the change).

Self-check before you answer: in STAGED MODE every file or symbol the message names must appear in `git diff --cached`. Delete any line that does not.

Always: base every statement on the actual diff. Never invent features, files, tests, or behaviour. Ignore pure noise (generated files, lockfiles, formatting-only churn) unless it is the whole change.

2. Format

<type>(<scope>): <summary>

<body>

<footer>
Co-Authored-By: Mohammed El Shora <riyadm2001@gmail.com>

- Title: Conventional Commits, lowercase type, imperative present tense ("add", not "added"), no trailing period, 72 characters or fewer.
- Types: feat, fix, refactor, perf, test, docs, build, ci, chore, style, revert.
- Scopes (pick the main one; omit if the change is repo-wide): text, phone, picker, attachments, theme, preview, example, docs, rules, deps, release.
- Exactly one blank line between title, body, and footer. Wrap body lines at 72 characters.
- Output ONLY the commit message. No code fences, no explanations, no greeting.

3. Body: complete and correct

- Start with one or two sentences saying what problem this solves or why the change exists.
- Then bullets, one per logical change. Each bullet states WHAT changed and WHY, naming the concrete class/function/file. Group by area when the diff spans several (for example "Text:", "Tests:", "Docs:").
- Cover everything that matters in the diff: behaviour changes, new public API (names and signatures in plain words), removed or renamed public API, new dependencies and why, tests added or changed, docs/README/CHANGELOG/pubspec version changes, config and rule changes.
- Public API removed or behaviour dropped: add a bullet beginning "Removed:" saying what hosts must change.
- Breaking change: add a footer line `BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and how to migrate>`.
- Mixed unrelated concerns: choose the dominant type for the title and cover every concern in the body. Do not hide any part of the diff.
- Mention how it was verified only if the diff shows it (tests added or updated).

4. Style

- English only. Plain, specific wording. No filler ("various", "some improvements", "minor tweaks").
- Do not mention AI, assistants, generators, or tools. No emojis.
- Do not paste code. Do not restate file lists without meaning.

5. Footer (always last)

The final line is exactly:
Co-Authored-By: Mohammed El Shora <riyadm2001@gmail.com>

Preceded by one blank line (or by `BREAKING CHANGE:` / issue reference lines, with the trailer after them). Write it once; never duplicate it.
